# 11. `/actuator/metrics`

Metrics tell us about the application's runtime behavior.

```text
GET /actuator/metrics
```

This returns available metric names.

Examples:

```text
http.server.requests
jvm.memory.used
jvm.threads.live
process.cpu.usage
system.cpu.usage
disk.free
disk.total
```

---

# 12. Individual Metrics

We can request a specific metric.

Example:

```text
GET /actuator/metrics/http.server.requests
```

This provides information about HTTP requests.

Another example:

```text
GET /actuator/metrics/jvm.memory.used
```

This provides JVM memory usage.

Other examples:

```text
GET /actuator/metrics/jvm.threads.live

GET /actuator/metrics/process.cpu.usage

GET /actuator/metrics/system.cpu.usage
```

---

# 13. `http.server.requests`

This is one of the most useful metrics for a REST application.

Suppose our banking application receives:

```text
GET /accounts/1
GET /accounts/1
GET /accounts/1
```

Actuator records HTTP request metrics.

For example:

```text
http.server.requests
        |
        +---- method
        +---- URI
        +---- status
        +---- outcome
        +---- exception
        +---- count
```

The `COUNT` measurement tells us how many requests have been recorded for the selected metric/tag combination.

---

# 14. Metrics and Tags

Metrics can have tags/dimensions.

For example:

```text
method = GET
status = 200
```

or:

```text
method = POST
status = 201
```

This allows us to analyze requests in more detail.

Conceptually:

```text
HTTP Requests
      |
      v
http.server.requests
      |
      +---- GET
      +---- POST
      |
      +---- 200
      +---- 404
      +---- 500
```

---

# 15. `/actuator/beans`

The beans endpoint shows the Spring beans registered in the ApplicationContext.

```text
GET /actuator/beans
```

For example, our banking application may contain:

```text
AccountController
CustomerController
AccountService
CustomerService
AccountRepo
CustomerRepo
DataSource
EntityManager
```

Conceptually:

```text
Spring ApplicationContext
        |
        +---- Controller beans
        +---- Service beans
        +---- Repository beans
        +---- DataSource
        +---- Other beans
```

The endpoint helps us inspect what Spring has created and manages.

---

# 16. What Information Does `/beans` Show?

Depending on the Spring Boot version, it can show information such as:

* Bean name
* Bean type
* Scope
* Dependencies
* Resource information

For example:

```json
{
  "accountController": {
    "type": "com.example.cruds.controllers.AccountController",
    "scope": "singleton",
    "dependencies": [
      "accountService"
    ]
  }
}
```

The exact structure varies.

---

# 17. `/actuator/mappings`

The mappings endpoint shows HTTP mappings registered by Spring MVC.

```text
GET /actuator/mappings
```

Suppose we have:

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/accounts")
public class AccountController {

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public AccountResponseDTO getAccount(...) {
        ...
    }

    @PostMapping
    public AccountResponseDTO createAccount(...) {
        ...
    }
}
```

Spring registers mappings such as:

```text
GET  /accounts/{id}
POST /accounts
```

Actuator lets us inspect those mappings.

---

# 18. Why `/mappings` Is Useful

Suppose we call:

```text
GET /accounts/10
```

and receive:

```text
404 Not Found
```

We can inspect:

```text
/actuator/mappings
```

and check whether Spring registered:

```text
GET /accounts/{id}
```

Maybe the actual mapping is:

```text
GET /api/accounts/{id}
```

This makes `/mappings` useful for debugging routing problems.

---

# 19. `/actuator/loggers`

The loggers endpoint allows us to inspect logger configuration.

```text
GET /actuator/loggers
```

It can also inspect a specific logger:

```text
GET /actuator/loggers/com.example.cruds.services.AccountService
```

Example:

```json
{
  "configuredLevel": "DEBUG",
  "effectiveLevel": "DEBUG"
}
```

---

# 20. Configured Level vs Effective Level

Suppose we have:

```yaml
logging:
  level:
    com.example.cruds: DEBUG
```

Then:

```text
com.example.cruds
        |
        v
      DEBUG
        |
        +---- services
        +---- controllers
        +---- repositories
```

A child logger can inherit the parent's logging level.

### Configured level

The level explicitly configured for that logger.

### Effective level

The level that actually applies to the logger after inheritance is considered.

---

# 21. Change Logging Level at Runtime

One of the useful features of `/loggers` is that we can change a logger's level without restarting the application.

Example:

```text
POST /actuator/loggers/com.example.cruds.services.AccountService
```

Request body:

```json
{
  "configuredLevel": "DEBUG"
}
```

Now the logger uses:

```text
DEBUG
```

without restarting the application.

---

# 22. Logging Level Example

Suppose our code contains:

```java
log.trace("Entering method");
log.debug("Account ID: {}", id);
log.info("Getting account");
log.warn("Account not found");
log.error("Database error");
```

If the level is:

```text
INFO
```

then:

```text
TRACE  ❌
DEBUG  ❌
INFO   ✅
WARN   ✅
ERROR  ✅
```

If we change it to:

```text
DEBUG
```

then:

```text
TRACE  ❌
DEBUG  ✅
INFO   ✅
WARN   ✅
ERROR  ✅
```

If we change it to:

```text
TRACE
```

everything is enabled.

---

# 23. Reset Runtime Logger Configuration

We can remove the runtime override by sending:

```text
POST /actuator/loggers/com.example.cruds.services.AccountService
```

with:

```json
{
  "configuredLevel": null
}
```

The logger can then inherit its level from its parent configuration again.

---

# 24. Actuator and Logging

Actuator does not replace SLF4J or Logback.

The normal logging flow is:

```text
Application Code
       |
       v
     SLF4J
       |
       v
    Logback
       |
       v
Console / File
```

Actuator provides management functionality alongside this:

```text
                    Application
                        |
             +----------+----------+
             |                     |
          Logging                Actuator
             |                     |
           SLF4J                 /loggers
             |                     |
          Logback             Runtime control
             |
       Console / File
```

