# 5. `/actuator/sessions`

### Purpose

The `sessions` endpoint provides session-related management when the application uses **Spring Session**.

```text
GET /actuator/sessions
```

### What is a session?

HTTP is stateless.

A session allows the server to associate multiple requests with the same user/session.

Conceptually:

```text
User
 ↓
Login
 ↓
Session created
 ↓
Session ID
 ↓
Subsequent requests
 ↓
Server identifies session
```

### Spring Session

Spring Session provides infrastructure for managing sessions.

Sessions can be stored using supported stores such as:

```text
Redis
JDBC
```

This can be useful when running multiple application instances:

```text
             Load Balancer
                  |
          +-------+-------+
          |               |
      App Instance 1  App Instance 2
          |               |
          +-------+-------+
                  |
                Redis
                  |
               Sessions
```

Both application instances can access the shared session store.

### Does our banking application need it?

Currently, **no**.

Our banking application is primarily a REST API application and we're moving toward Spring Security.

If we use a stateless token-based approach such as JWT, we don't need a server-side HTTP session for each request.

Conceptually:

```text
Session-based

Client
  ↓
Session ID
  ↓
Server Session
```

versus:

```text
Token-based

Client
  ↓
JWT
  ↓
Server validates token
```

We'll learn this properly during Spring Security.

